  "pull_request": {
    "number": 152,
    "title": "Docs/UI test blessing",
    "body": "## What\r\nDocuments CONTRIBUTING.md's UI test model: where cases live (`soroban_cost_lints/ui/main.rs`),\r\nwhere expected output is checked in (`soroban_cost_lints/ui/main.stderr`), how to run just that\r\ntest, and how to regenerate `main.stderr` correctly.\r\n\r\n## Why\r\nThe old \"Adding a New Lint\" section stopped at \"write a failing test case,\" with no mention that\r\n`main.stderr` is a checked-in golden file that must be regenerated by hand when a lint's message\r\nor span changes. New contributors were hand-editing it to make the diff go away instead.\r\n\r\n## Key finding\r\nThe obvious `BLESS=1 cargo test` approach (common in dylint/clippy projects) is a **no-op** on the\r\nversions pinned here — `dylint_testing = \"6.0.1\"` (wrapping `compiletest_rs = \"0.11.2\"` per\r\nCargo.lock) never reads a BLESS env var or sets `compiletest::Config.bless`; I traced this in the\r\nactual pinned crate source rather than relying on general dylint documentation, since that env var\r\ndoes exist in newer releases of these crates and would have been a plausible but wrong thing to\r\ndocument. The real mechanism, confirmed from `compiletest_rs::runtest::compare_output`: on a\r\nmismatch it always writes the actual output to a temp-dir path and prints\r\n`Actual stderr saved to <path>`, which is what needs copying over the checked-in file.\r\n\r\n## Testing\r\nDocumentation-only change — `cargo fmt` / `cargo clippy` / `cargo test` are unaffected by content.\r\nI manually walked through the documented regeneration steps against the pinned\r\n`dylint_testing`/`compiletest_rs` source before writing them up.\r\n\r\nCloses #89",
